commit 62b0b4d51b05ff84cd39f2cb19a9dc09eef9a49a
Author: gevik <gevikb@gmail.com>
Date:   Wed Nov 25 14:21:16 2026 +0100

    fix(semantic): demand the bytes the chip fetches, not the object around them
    
    The inferred sprite-visibility demand was stated against the object root and
    enforced over every byte of it, so an object larger than a visible interval had
    no legal home even though the chip fetches 64 bytes of it. Two ordinary
    5,000-byte sprite sources were refused together for that reason: each fits
    alone, and only the pair cannot be visible whole.
    
    The demand now carries the effective offset and the fetched extent, and layout
    checks those runs instead of the object. Measured, same profile and programs as
    the recorded baseline:
    
      9,000-byte sheet, helper and inline     E10273 -> $0A80, block visible
      two 5,000-byte sheets, own helpers      E10273 -> $0AC0 and $2000, both visible
      published after a definite overwrite    E10273 -> $0AC0
      hi(&buffer / 64), helper and inline     E10273 -> $0AC0
      poke-only, runtime index, 64B control   unchanged addresses
    
    The second 5,000-byte object is the whole argument: it used to sit at $1E40,
    inside the window where the chip reads character ROM, building successfully with
    a silently invisible sprite. It is now at $2000 and the program builds. Dropping
    the demand would restore acceptance and leave that sprite invisible; the
    whole-object demand kept it visible by refusing the program. Only a demand that
    names the fetched bytes does both.
    
    Three further defects in the same seam, each reachable on its own:
    
    A runtime index proves alignment for every element and names none, so the run
    widens to the object rather than shrinking to the element the static prefix
    happens to name. Shrinking there is the one change in this unit that could turn
    an unnecessary refusal into a sprite the chip cannot see.
    
    Only an operator that leaves the selector naming the same block now carries
    provenance. hi(address / 64) is a value in 0..3 selecting a block in the first
    256 bytes of the bank, not one inside the object whose address produced it.
    
    A value this body provably replaces stops the walk: same block, later, same
    statically known bytes, and a home no other body or interrupt can read. A
    conditional replacement and a module variable both keep the demand, because the
    proof follows neither branch nor other bodies.
    
    The refusal that remains - a 16 KiB array every runtime index can select - now
    names the sprite-block expression it was inferred from instead of telling the
    author to remove an explicit constraint they never wrote. spec/ is untouched;
    the handler-visibility branch beside it already departs from that frozen tail
    for the same reason, and the pinned canonical framing covers explicitly placed
    objects only.
    
    What the chip fetches through each VIC field now has one owner in target/,
    re-exported where lowering already reached for it.
    
    Package 267 files / 4,570 tests. Host tier 281 files / 6,029. Impl tier 44 cases
    in the pass and 149 in layout. Byte identity 9/9 across three examples and three
    optimization goals.

